  "question": {
    "text": "The number of radial node/s for <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mn>3</mn><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">p</mi></math> orbital is:"
  },
  "options": [
    {
      "text": "<math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mn>1</mn></math>",
      "is_correct": true
    },
    {
      "text": "<math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mn>4</mn></math>",
      "is_correct": false
    },
    {
      "text": "<math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mn>2</mn></math>",
      "is_correct": false
    },
    {
      "text": "<math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mn>3</mn></math>",
      "is_correct": false
    }
  ],
  "correct_answer": [
    "A"
  ],
  "solution": {
    "text": "<p>A point or plane of an orbital where the electron density of the electron of an orbital is zero is called a node. Nodes can be two types; one is a radial node and another one is an angular node.</p><p>Number of radial node<math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mo>=</mo><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">n</mi><mo>-</mo><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">l</mi><mo>-</mo><mn>1</mn></math></p><p>Now, for <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mn>3</mn><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">p</mi></math> orbital, the value of <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">n</mi></math> is <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mn>3</mn></math>, and the azimuthal quantum number is <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mn>1</mn></math>. Therefore, the number of radial node is, <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">n</mi><mo>-</mo><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">l</mi><mo>-</mo><mn>1</mn><mo>=</mo><mo>(</mo><mn>3</mn><mo>-</mo><mn>1</mn><mo>-</mo><mn>1</mn><mo>)</mo><mo>=</mo><mn>1</mn></math></p><p>Hence, ans will be <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">A</mi></math></p>"
  }
